🌿 Terraform Variables, Outputs, Locals & Expressions


---

🎯 Why Variables?

Without variables, Terraform files become hardcoded and repetitive.

Variables help make your code dynamic, reusable, and maintainable —
you can change values without editing your main code.


---

🧩 1. Variables in Terraform


---

🔹 Syntax

You declare variables using the variable block.

variable "instance_type" {
  description = "EC2 instance type"
  type        = string
  default     = "t2.micro"
}

Then, you reference it like this:

instance_type = var.instance_type


---

🔹 Variable Types

Type	Example	Notes

string	"t2.micro"	Text values
number	8080	Numeric values
bool	true / false	Boolean values
list	["t2.micro", "t2.small"]	Ordered collection
map	{ env = "dev", region = "ap-south-1" }	Key-value pairs
object	object({name=string, size=number})	Custom structure
tuple	[ "dev", 1, true ]	Mixed-type list



---

🧱 Example 1 — Simple Variable

variable "aws_region" {
  description = "AWS region"
  type        = string
  default     = "ap-south-1"
}

provider "aws" {
  region = var.aws_region
}


---

🧱 Example 2 — List Variable

variable "availability_zones" {
  type = list(string)
  default = ["ap-south-1a", "ap-south-1b"]
}

resource "aws_subnet" "example" {
  count             = length(var.availability_zones)
  vpc_id            = aws_vpc.main.id
  cidr_block        = cidrsubnet(var.vpc_cidr, 8, count.index)
  availability_zone = var.availability_zones[count.index]
}

✅ Dynamically creates subnets in each AZ.


---

🧱 Example 3 — Map Variable

variable "env_tags" {
  type = map(string)
  default = {
    env     = "dev"
    project = "terraform-demo"
  }
}

resource "aws_instance" "example" {
  ami           = "ami-0abcd1234"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"

  tags = var.env_tags
}

✅ Automatically applies consistent tags to all resources.


---

📘 2. Variable Input Methods

You can set variable values in several ways (priority order):

Priority	Method	Example

1️⃣	CLI flag	terraform apply -var="instance_type=t2.small"
2️⃣	tfvars file	terraform.tfvars or dev.tfvars
3️⃣	Environment variable	export TF_VAR_instance_type=t2.micro
4️⃣	Default value	Inside the variable block



---

🧱 Example — terraform.tfvars

aws_region     = "us-east-1"
instance_type  = "t3.micro"
env            = "dev"

Terraform automatically picks this file if named terraform.tfvars.


---

🧠 3. Locals (Local Variables)


---

🌍 Definition:

locals are temporary variables used inside the same configuration —
useful for simplifying expressions or avoiding repetition.


---

🧱 Example

locals {
  common_tags = {
    environment = "dev"
    owner       = "Vamsi"
  }

  app_name = "demo-app"
}

resource "aws_instance" "app" {
  ami           = "ami-0abcd1234"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
  tags = merge(local.common_tags, {
    Name = local.app_name
  })
}

✅ Cleaner, DRY code — you define once, reuse everywhere.


---

📤 4. Outputs


---

🌍 Definition:

Outputs display important resource details after terraform apply.
They help you retrieve values like public IPs, URLs, etc.


---

🧱 Example

output "instance_ip" {
  description = "Public IP of EC2"
  value       = aws_instance.example.public_ip
}

✅ After terraform apply, Terraform will show:

Outputs:
instance_ip = "3.111.145.92"


---

🧱 Output with Sensitive Value

output "db_password" {
  value     = var.db_password
  sensitive = true
}

✅ Hides the value from terminal output (for security).


---

🔣 5. Terraform Expressions


---

Expressions let you combine variables, functions, and logic.


---

🧱 Interpolation

You can refer to variables or resource attributes using ${} syntax:

ami = "${data.aws_ami.amazon_linux.id}"

✅ In modern Terraform, ${} is optional — can just write:

ami = data.aws_ami.amazon_linux.id


---

🧠 Arithmetic & Logical Expressions

count = var.env == "prod" ? 3 : 1

✅ If environment is prod → 3 instances, else 1.


---

🧩 String Interpolation

tags = {
  Name = "web-${var.env}"
}

✅ Generates dynamic tag names (e.g., web-dev).


---

🧮 6. Terraform Built-in Functions

Function	Example	Description

length()	length(var.subnets)	Returns number of elements
upper() / lower()	upper(var.env)	Changes case
merge()	merge(map1, map2)	Combine maps
join()	join(",", var.list)	Joins list into string
lookup()	lookup(var.map, "key", "default")	Safe map access
cidrsubnet()	cidrsubnet("10.0.0.0/16", 8, 1)	Derive subnet from CIDR
file()	file("key.pem")	Reads file content
format()	format("Instance-%s", var.env)	String formatting
toset(), tomap(), tolist()	Convert between data types	



---

🔐 7. Sensitive Variables

If you want to hide sensitive info (like passwords):

variable "db_password" {
  type      = string
  sensitive = true
}

✅ Terraform will mask it in outputs and CLI logs.


---

🧩 Summary Table

Concept	Purpose	Example

variable	Input value from user/config	region = var.aws_region
locals	Internal reusable variables	local.common_tags
output	Display values after apply	output “instance_ip”
expressions	Combine values dynamically	“web-${var.env}”
functions	Built-in helpers for logic	length(), merge(), lookup()



---

💡 Real Example Putting All Together

variable "env" {
  default = "dev"
}

variable "instance_type" {
  default = "t2.micro"
}

locals {
  tags = {
    environment = var.env
    owner       = "Vamsi"
  }
}

resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami           = data.aws_ami.amazon_linux.id
  instance_type = var.instance_type
  tags          = local.tags
}

output "instance_ip" {
  value = aws_instance.web.public_ip
}

✅ Clean, dynamic, and reusable Terraform configuration!
